# Project context

Follow the repository charter in **`AGENTS.md`** at the project root for shared conventions, security expectations, and the canonical **`.ai-agents/`** layout (skills, agents, commands, hooks).
This repository is domain-agnostic; do not add product-domain logic into shared agent assets.

**MUST, local-first precedence:** When the workspace root has its own rules, templates, or conventions, follow those first; this toolkit is the fallback. Order: session instruction, then workspace-root rules (`AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.cursor/rules/`), then conventions already in the consumer repo, then `.ai-agents/`. Detect before assuming; on conflict follow the local rule and state the divergence (see **`AGENTS.md`**, Local-first precedence).

**MUST, authoring:** When creating a new skill, subagent, command, or hook under **`.ai-agents/`**, follow that folder's **`TEMPLATE.md`** and complete all sections (What, Why, How, When, Routing & discovery, Permissions & authority).

**MUST, routing:** Before choosing which existing asset to use, read **`.ai-agents/ROUTER.md`** and the appropriate subfolder **`ROUTER.md`** (for example `skills/ROUTER.md`).

**MUST, router tables:** After creating, renaming, or deleting any asset under `.ai-agents/skills`, `agents`, `commands`, or `hooks`, update that folder's **`ROUTER.md`** in the same change so the table lists current files, use cases, and notes.

**MUST, commit attribution:** Never add AI/agent co-author trailers (`Co-Authored-By: ...`), "Generated with ...", or robot-emoji attribution to commits or PR bodies, including from `/build` and `/ship`. Attribute every commit only to the human's git identity (see **`AGENTS.md`**, Commit attribution).

**MUST, commit messages:** Use a human-friendly conventional message `type(scope): subject` (feat, fix, chore, refactor, and so on) that matches the branch name. Plain words, no AI-tell filler, no emojis/icons, no em-dash.

**MUST, clarify first:** When a request is ambiguous, underspecified, or has conflicting constraints, ask the user before changing code. Do not guess and execute.

**MUST, plain writing:** In code, comments, commit messages, and replies, write plain human language. Add only necessary, meaningful comments (explain why, not what). Do not use AI-tell filler words (ensure, enhance, simplify, leverage, utilize, seamless, robust, comprehensive). Do not use emojis, icons, or the em-dash character.

**MUST, read project docs:** Before using or upgrading a framework/library, read the docs for the version pinned in this repo's manifests/lockfiles, not memory. Ask if the version is unclear.

**Permissions:** See **`.ai-agents/PERMISSIONS.md`** and [Claude Code permissions](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions) for how **`.claude/settings.json`** relates to documented tool needs.

For Cursor-specific paths and linking skills after clone, see **`CURSOR.md`**.

Do not duplicate long policy in this rule file; update **`AGENTS.md`** or **`.ai-agents/README.md`** instead.

## Always-on behavioral baseline

- Apply **`karpathy-guardrails`** for assumptions, simplicity, scoped edits, verification before completion, and grounded claims.
- **Grounded claims (no fabrication):** never describe a file, path, command result, or source you have not actually opened, listed, or run; report `ACCESS-FAILED: <path>` for inaccessible inputs instead of inferring structure. Applies to primary agents and subagents alike.
- Apply **`token-efficient-execution`** for concise default responses and low-noise execution summaries.
- If user asks for deep explanation, tradeoffs, or teaching mode, prioritize that request over concision.
